metal2vulkan (Mi A3, iOS 26): the runtime's threads take Metal's classes on iOS as on macOS (the code was there behind target_os = macos): the threads that bring a command buffer to its handlers at user-interactive, the compile threads at user-initiated.
Then the scheduler and the handler pool take the scheduling policy and priority of the first thread that makes a command buffer at a fixed policy (CoreAnimation's render thread, priority 54 here): a user-interactive thread is timeshare and its priority decays under load (AdrenoMetal 241f355: 47 to 37, 53 of 78 slow-call samples on a run queue). The render thread waits for the scheduler in waitUntilScheduled. The queue's wait threads keep the class only: a fence-call loop there must not run ahead of the render thread.
One line: LAUREL RENDER CLASS: on|off. Kill switch: NEGRONIMETAL_QOS=0 (classes and policy).
